  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three ways electrochemical charge can be stored at an electrode. In all cases, ions in an electrolyte interact with a solid surface, but the mechanism differs.\n\nOn the left, the EDLC case shows charge stored physically at a carbon surface. Anions and cations gather near the electrode to form an electric double layer, with no obvious chemical transformation of the solid. This emphasizes surface adsorption and electrostatic separation of charge.\n\nThe middle panel shows redox pseudocapacitance. Here, ions approach a redox-active material and charge is stored through fast surface or near-surface redox reactions, indicated by a reversible reaction between oxidized and reduced states. The ions are associated with electron transfer rather than just electrostatic accumulation.\n\nThe right panel shows intercalation pseudocapacitance. Lithium ions move into a layered or lattice-like host structure, occupying sites within the material. This still represents rapid charge storage, but now the ions enter the bulk host rather than remaining only at the surface.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights a progression from purely electrostatic storage, to surface redox storage, to ion insertion into a solid framework.",
